---
title: Installation
description: Install Frame on macOS, Windows, or Linux and keep it up to date.
order: 10
---

Download the current release from [Frame on GitHub](https://github.com/66HEX/frame/releases/latest). Choose the package for your operating system and processor. Release packages include the media tools they need; the Flatpak uses the Freedesktop runtime.

## macOS

Use the Apple Silicon package for an M-series Mac, or the Intel package for an Intel Mac. Open the DMG and move Frame to Applications.

You can also install through the Frame Homebrew tap:

```bash
brew tap 66HEX/frame
brew install --cask frame
```

The current macOS release is ad-hoc signed and is not notarized. If macOS blocks a copy downloaded from the official release page, the repository documents this command after installation:

```bash
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Frame.app
```

Only use it for the official Frame download you intended to install.

## Windows

Download the Windows installer from the release page and run it. Frame is also available through WinGet:

```bash
winget install --id 66HEX.Frame -e
```

Current Windows builds are not certificate signed. SmartScreen may show a warning. For the official installer, select **More info**, then **Run anyway**.

## Linux

Install from Flathub:

```bash
flatpak install flathub io.github._66HEX.Frame
```

AppImage downloads are also available on the GitHub release page. Choose x86_64 for a typical Intel or AMD computer, or aarch64 for an ARM64 computer, then allow the downloaded file to run as an executable in your file manager.

### Nixpkgs

Frame is available as [`frame-media-converter` in Nixpkgs](https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/blob/nixos-unstable/pkgs/by-name/fr/frame-media-converter/package.nix), on the `nixos-unstable` branch, for x86_64 and aarch64 Linux. This package uses FFmpeg and FFprobe from Nixpkgs. Updates are managed through Nix.

With Nix installed and the [`nix-command` and `flakes` features enabled](https://nix.dev/concepts/flakes#running-commands), run Frame without adding it to your profile:

```bash
nix run github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable#frame-media-converter
```

To install it in your user profile:

```bash
nix profile add github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable#frame-media-converter
```

On older Nix versions, use `nix profile install` in place of `nix profile add`. After installation, launch Frame from your application menu or run `frame` in a terminal.

For a NixOS configuration that already uses `nixos-unstable`, add `pkgs.frame-media-converter` to your existing `environment.systemPackages` list:

```nix
{ pkgs, ... }: {
  environment.systemPackages = [ pkgs.frame-media-converter ];
}
```

Then apply your configuration with `sudo nixos-rebuild switch`. The package must come from `nixos-unstable`; the commands above select that branch explicitly, even if your default Nixpkgs source uses a stable release. See the [NixOS package management guide](https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/#sec-package-management) for declarative installation.

## Updates

Native release builds include an updater. Frame verifies the release manifest signature and the downloaded package before installation. If you installed through a package manager, you can update through that same package manager:

```bash
brew upgrade --cask frame
```

```bash
winget upgrade --id 66HEX.Frame -e
```

```bash
flatpak update io.github._66HEX.Frame
```

For an installation made with `nix profile add`:

```bash
nix profile upgrade frame-media-converter
```

If you have more than one copy in your profile, use `nix profile list` to find the correct entry name. For declarative NixOS installations, update your `nixos-unstable` channel or flake input and rebuild the configuration.

After installation, continue with [your first conversion](/docs/getting-started).
